faq:
  - q: "How do I explain a career break in an interview?"
    a: "Directly, briefly, and without apology — then pivot to your readiness. The best answer follows this structure: state the reason honestly (caregiving, health, family relocation, sabbatical), add one thing that kept you engaged during the break (relevant course, freelance project, staying current in your field), and close with your current readiness and focus. Example: 'I took two years away to care for a family member. During that time, I completed [course] and stayed current in [field]. I'm now fully available and ready to return — I'm specifically looking for [role type] at [company type] where I can [specific value].'"
  - q: "How do I update my resume after a long career break?"
    a: "Four adjustments: (1) Use a hybrid resume format — skills and summary at the top, then experience. This leads with your capabilities before the gap becomes visible. (2) Do not hide the gap — include a brief line for the career break period ('Career Break — Caregiving / Relocation / Personal Health') to close the white space with honesty rather than confusion. (3) Update every section to modern standards — remove outdated formatting, old software references, and anything that signals 2010. (4) Expand the Skills section to reflect any courses, certifications, or self-study from the break period."
  - q: "How long is too long of a career break?"
    a: "There is no universal threshold — what matters is how you position it, not the duration. Professionals have successfully returned to senior roles after 3, 5, even 10 years away when the return narrative was clear, the skills were refreshed, and the positioning was tailored. The harder cases are: gaps of 5+ years in fast-moving technical fields (software, AI) where skills decay quickly; and senior roles where 'gap' becomes confused with 'performance departure.' Both are solvable — but require more deliberate repositioning."
  - q: "What is the fastest way to re-enter the job market after a career break?"
    a: "Three channels work fastest for returnees: (1) Returnship programs — structured re-entry programs at large companies (Amazon, Apple, Goldman Sachs, and others) that are specifically designed for professionals with career gaps. These convert at very high rates because the employer is self-selected for openness to gaps. (2) Former colleagues and managers — your pre-break network is the fastest path to a warm introduction. Contact former managers and colleagues before doing anything else. (3) Contract or freelance work first — even a 3-month consulting engagement or contract role resets your gap end date and refreshes your narrative."
  - q: "Do I need to retrain or take courses to return to work?"
    a: "Depends on the field and the length of the gap. For most management, strategy, and non-technical roles: a targeted refresh (reading current industry content, completing a relevant course, updating tools knowledge) is sufficient. For technical roles (software engineering, data science): gaps of 2+ years typically require active skill refreshing — specific tools and languages change fast enough that a 3+ year gap in technical currency is noticeable in interviews. A focused 4–8 week preparation sprint often closes the gap."
